<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../Graphics/Australia_20050921.jpg"><img class="PicBorder" src="Graphics/AustraliaSmall_20050921.jpg" align="right"></a>
<u>11-SEP-2005 11:01 PM </u><br>
Today I would be doing something you can't do anywhere else in the world (at least as of the date of this log entry).  Today, I would be climbing the Sydney Harbour Bridge (www.bridgeclimb.com) and earn one of the best views in Sydney.  M was going to wait around Sydney Cove while I get done. The entire climb takes about 3.5 hrs to do.  The weather wasn't looking so good as it was raining in the morning and I had thought about trying to call the bridgeclimb office to change my times but I read on the tickets that you can't change the times. You go rain or shine unless they deem unsafe.  Drats. I was in for long haul.
<p>
Anyway, I got off the train at the Circular Quay stop and head to the Bridgeclimb office on Cumberland Street. On my way there I had noticed a just a lack of life in the city. Yeah, it was early and Sunday, but where were the people??? It was still a big city.  Just after thinking that, I turned around and noticed and endless line of marathon runners going to the Sydney Opera House.  Of course!!! Ching had reminded me that today was the marathon and that explains the lack of traffic and people roaming about the city.
<p>
As I arrived at the Bridgeclimb office, I had about 30 minutes to spare so I stared at the Wall of Fame.  TV/Music/Movie/Sports personalities as well as royalty who had done the Bridgeclimb.  Today I would join the ranks of such famous people as Will Smith, Matt Damon, Laurence Fishburne, Prince Henry and Fergi Duchess of York.
<p>
The time finally came up and we entered a small room where we were forced to take a breathalizer test and fill out some paperwork before going to a dressing room to change into jumpsuits.  We were not allowed to have anything in our pockets or on our necks/hands/arms for the fear of them falling off and onto travelling cars below the bridge.  Also, the jumpsuits were gun metal gray, the same colour as the bridge itself.  I'm told this is so we are not distracting to the drivers.
<p>
Following the gowning up, we put on a belt that contained a pulley wheel that latched onto a metal twisted guide wire.   We also latched on a hanky, a hat, eyeglass holders (if you wear glasses like I do), and a fleece jacket.  Then we had to go through a simulation stairway containing a very steep ladder to climb up and down to simulate the hardest part of the climb.  Finally, we were given radios and bone-jarring waterproof headsets (the same used by the Navy Seals) so that the tour guide to talk to us and we could all hear her.
<p>
We walked over to the start point and latched our pulleys to the guide wire and started the ascent.  Wow, the weather had changed completely from raining to nice and sunny with blue skies and some clouds.  I couldn't ask for better weather.  The beginning had us climbing under the bridge while the cars and trains were overhead.  Then we climbed up the most difficult part via the steep ladders and we were now on top of the bridge walking up small slightly angled steps until we reached the top.  What a view.  I could see out for maybe 50-60 km.  I could even view the arch of the Sydney Olympic stadium.
<p>
We had crossed to the apex of the bridge and it was time to turn around onto the other side of the bridge and head back down the way we came.  The trip was a lot of fun and I would recommend it but it is quite a lot of $$$ to pay so be warned.  You can also do the Pylon Looking for only $8.50 but it's just not quite the same as the bridgeclimb though you still get a pretty good view for a lot lot less. If your only objective is to get a good view, then I would say just do the Pylon Lookout.
<p>
With half of the day gone now, we decided on doing just two more things -- The Sydney Olympic Stadium and Bondi Beach.  We hopped on a fast cat to Sydney Olympic Park. It was quite a ways out there and when we got off at the dock we caught a bus to take us there.  However, when we got there, it was dead!  Hardly anyone around and not much really open.  Bored to death, we took our picks and decided to leave so we can do Bondi Beach.
<p>
The fastest way to Bondi Beach was by train and then bus and that's exactly what we did.  Bondi Beach is a popular area where Australian celebrities live.  Kinda like Beverly Hills.  When we finally arrived at Bondi Beach it was crowded and very touristy and alls sorts of souvenir shops and restaurants and bars lined up on the street by the beach.  On the beach itself, people were flying kites and surfing.  Yes, you could rent a board and a suit and surf.
<p>
Pretty much exhausted for the day, we decided we would just go to Chinatown and head back to Ching's place and call it a good trip in Sydney.  As we got back to Chings, I found out that Robert had gotten a place to stay and a cell phone and was on his way to settling down in Sydney for a little while so he had already moved out of Ching's place.  Robert, if you are reading this, I hope your journeys go well and take care my friend.
<p>
Ching and M and I basically sat down and watched an episode of Australian Idol before going to bed. Tomorrow we would begin our 2 day drive to Melbourne.
<p>
   
